00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  welcome to the google cloud  security showcase a special web series  where we will focus on security use  cases  that customers can solve with google  cloud  my name is megan yahya and i'm product  manager  at google cloud today we'll be walking  through one of the top questions we get  from customers  how can i detect network-based threats  in my google cloud environment  cloud ideas is google cloud's  network-based threat detection  technology  that detects malware spyware command and  control attacks  and many other classes of exploits  it provides fully managed network threat  detection  with high levels of security efficacy  and low noise  it's quick and easy to deploy fully  managed  scaled and operated by google with high  performance  and availability built in we have  partnered  with our friends at palo alto networks  to embed  their industry-leading threat detection  technologies  to make sure we have leading detection  both in breadth of coverage and efficacy  backed by their expert production proven  technologies  and world-class threat intelligence  teams  here is a proud overview of how cloud  ideas works  cloud ideas is a fully managed cloud  native product  that receives the mirrored traffic from  customers network  and runs threat detection technology  from palo alto networks  under the hood cloud ideas may be  applied  via packet mirroring to any and all  traffic  to or from a workload whether it's a  compute engine in gce  or a container in gke cloud ideas  moves threat detection closer to the  workload  not just vpc to internet or vpc to vpc  but also intravpc traffic are inspected  for full north south and east-west  visibility and detection  this dramatically simplifies the  fulfillment of the ideas requirement  common to several compliance marks it  also fills in the east-west  blind spots suffered by most socs  in cloud environments cloud ideas  also integrates with soar which is the  security orchestration  and automated response solutions such as  palo alto network's  xor product this enables customers  to set up automated threat responses  based on cloud ideas as alerts  we have built integrations with  third-party siem solutions  which provide security information and  event management to our customers  leveraging these partner solutions in  conjunction with cloud ideas  gives customers additional visibility  into network threats  and security analytics on cloud ideas  alerts  we are also building integrations with  chronicles security analytics platform  and scc the security management platform  on gcp  now let me show you how you can use  cloud ideas  to detect network-based threats in your  google cloud  environment here is how cloud ideas  looks like  inside gcp environment it is located  under network security section next to  cloud armor  and ssl policies products once you click  on cloud ideas  it browse you to the dashboard page  where you can see a summary of top  threads  and ip addresses that have been detected  within your alerts you can customize the  dashboard  using the time filters at the top by  selecting the time period  you're interested in  in order to get started you first need  to create cloud ids endpoints  an endpoint is a cluster of vms that are  running palo alto network's  threat detection technology under the  hood  so first i enter the endpoint name  then enter the network that you want to  inspect  if you have a shared vpc this would be  the host project  cloud ids creates a paired vpc network  attached to the network you specify here  the next step allows you to specify  minimum threat severity alerts you would  like to see  such as low and above  where low is for minor threats with  little impact  on organizations infrastructure and  critical  is a serious alert  once you create this endpoint you're  gonna see it under endpoints tab  now you have an ids endpoint that is  ready to receive  and inspect traffic and generate threats  but it is not receiving  any traffic yet until you attach a  packet mirroring policy to it  that will mirror the traffic from the  associated vpc  click attach to test to set up your  packet mirroring policy  now traffic from the specified source is  mirrored to the ids endpoint for  inspection  so you give the policy a name  and you pick the mirrored sources  and you press submit  the policy is going to show up next to  your endpoint  such as this policy that i had created  before  and that's it now you have your end  point and you have the packet mirroring  policy  so the traffic is flowing to the end  point  and the detection is happening so you  can go to the threads tab  to see the reported threads the time  filter on top allows you to view threads  for a specific time period  you can also use the filter menu to  filter by other thread properties such  as severity  or source ip addresses  you can now click on a specific thread  to get  more details about that thread  once you click on view thread details  it's going to take you to details page  stretch details page  also shows the application based on  application identification  technology app id it also shows you  information about the cv  severity threat type and more  description about the alert  if you go back you can see that you  alternatively you can clear click on  view thread logs  that will take you to a new tab with  threat information  in cloud logging cloud logging  also enables downstream integrations  with your same tools  such as splunk or soar tools such as  palo alto  xor you can also create custom workflows  to take remediation action based on  alerts such as creating a cloud function  that triggers on an alert and generates  a firewall rule to block the ip address  thank you for tuning in please visit  cloud.google.com  forward slash security for more content  from google cloud experts

